; Duke Dungeon — the endless descent: a map drawn from a seed every time it is played.
;
; Nothing here is a floor. It is what the generator is asked for, so the same seed is
; the same floor on every machine, and a new seed is somewhere nobody has been. Killing
; the boss opens the way down; each depth is the same generator with a new seed and
; tougher inhabitants, and the hero keeps what he has earned, so the two curves have to
; be tuned against each other.
ProceduralMap
  Name = Endless

  Generation = Layout
    ; Cells across and down. Kept tight on purpose: space beyond what the rooms
    ; need just becomes corridor, and corridor is walked through, not played.
    MapWidth = 50
    MapHeight = 36

    ; MinRooms is what the generator aims for, and it can only be a number that
    ; actually fits: rooms are placed by trying and rejecting, and one that will
    ; not go in is one the floor does without. Six is what always fits at these
    ; sizes — asking for ten got eight, which made the file claim a floor it could
    ; not keep. DungeonGeneratorTest holds the claim to its word.
    MinRooms = 6
    MaxRooms = 15
    MinRoomSize = 7
    MaxRoomSize = 13

    ; Stone left between rooms so a wall reads as a wall rather than a shared line.
    RoomGap = 1
    ; How many times to try landing a room before settling for the ones that fit.
    PlacementAttempts = 3000

    ; Corridor width in cells. Must be wide enough for the largest creature to walk
    ; down: a cell is 10 world units and the boss is 16 across, so one cell would
    ; wedge it. A mover with nowhere to swerve is how units used to end up inside
    ; walls, so this is a correctness setting rather than a matter of taste.
    CorridorWidth = 2

    ; How far a new room may be from the nearest one already placed, in cells.
    ; Keeps the dungeon compact: rooms scattered to opposite corners are joined by
    ; long empty corridors, which are the part a player walks rather than plays.
    MaxRoomSpacing = 30

    ; Per room other than the hero's. Every occupied room gets at least the minimum.
    MinSkeletonsPerRoom = 2
    MaxSkeletonsPerRoom = 6

    ; ---- height ----
    ;
    ; Rooms stand on storeys and the corridors between them run flat, at the
    ; storey the way in is on. Every climb happens at a doorway: walking out of a
    ; raised room you come down a step into the corridor. That is not a look — two
    ; corridors that crossed at different storeys would be a wall across both of
    ; them, built by nobody and invisible on the map.
    ;
    ; The highest a room may stand. 0 turns height off entirely and the dungeon is
    ; the flat one this game had before.
    MaxStorey = 2

    ; How often a room stands a storey away from the one before it, as a
    ; percentage. Every other room, roughly.
    StoreyChangePercent = 45

    ; How many cells of corridor one storey of stair takes. One cell is a proper
    ; flight; two is a gentle ramp — and a correctness setting as much as a taste
    ; one, because a corridor is measured centre to centre and most of it is room.
    ; A climb that will not fit in the corridor is cut down until it does, so a
    ; long stair means fewer rooms actually end up raised.
    StairLength = 1

    ; Where the hero comes in, and where the boss waits. The boss is asked for the
    ; top and takes what the corridor into his room can hold, so on a floor whose
    ; rooms sit close together he may be a storey lower.
    EntranceStorey = 0
    BossStorey = 2

    ; How the floor rises and falls over its storeys, in steps of a sixteenth of a
    ; cell: SAGE's height map, a whole number at every corner. Up to 15 and
    ; never steeper than a body can walk, since a cell is a cliff from 16 steps
    ; between its corners. 0 lays every floor flat on its storey.
    Hills = 6
    ; About how many cells across a hill is.
    HillSize = 4
  End

  ; ---- which depth wears which ----
  ;
  ; Two floors out of doors, then two of stone, then round again. Out of doors
  ; first because the first floor is the one everybody sees and nobody chose to
  ; see -- the descent reads better as going in than as going further in.
  ;
  ; WhenExhausted is the answer to a real question in a game with no bottom:
  ; Repeat begins the list over, Last stays in the deepest look for ever.
  Themes = [Forest, Forest, Dungeon, Dungeon]
  WhenExhausted = Repeat

  ; ---- what stands about in the rooms ----
  ;
  ; A room with nothing in it is a floor with a fight on it. These are ordinary
  ; templates (data/props/) with a shape and no body, which means the engine makes
  ; them solid — paths go round them, bodies stop at them — and neither brain will
  ; ever shoot at one, because both look for a candidate with a body. How each is
  ; drawn is the theme's business: a ThemeMonster in its Monsters re-skins any
  ; template, and a prop is a template. A theme that names no model for one gets
  ; the engine's plain shape, which for a cylinder is a pillar already.
  ;
  ; Fewest and most per room, including the one the hero starts in. Kept low:
  ; things to walk round are furniture, and a room full of furniture is a room
  ; you cannot fight in.
  PropsPerRoom = [0, 3]

  Descent = Descent
    ; ---- where it ends ----
    ;
    ; One boss per floor, in order, and this list is ALSO how many floors there
    ; are: kill the last of them and the run is won. There is no second number
    ; saying how deep the dungeon goes, because a second number is a number that
    ; can disagree with this one.
    ;
    ; Naming none puts the descent back the way it was: one boss called Boss, the
    ; same fight every floor, going down for ever. That is what this game did
    ; before it had an ending, and what a floor is worth depends entirely on the
    ; difference -- a descent with no bottom asks only how much further, and four
    ; named things to get past is a route you can finish.
    Bosses = [Warden, Reaper, Necromancer, Champion]

    ; ---- who stands with them ----
    ;
    ; A kind and how many of it. A kind not yet at its MinDepth stays away, so the
    ; first two bosses still wait alone and the last two have the green and the purple
    ; mages about them: two to mend the boss and two to fill the room while they do.
    ; They stand on a square ring BossGuardRing cells out from the boss, corners first,
    ; and further out if the room has no floor there. MaxPerRoom is for rooms the draw
    ; fills and does not apply here. Leave BossGuards out and every boss waits alone.
    BossGuards
      SkeletonHealer = 2
      SkeletonSummoner = 2
    End
    BossGuardRing = 2

    ; Percentages are of the creature's base value, added per depth beyond the
    ; first: at depth 3 with 25%, a monster has 1 + 2 x 0.25 = 1.5x health.
    MonsterHealthPercentPerDepth = 25
    MonsterDamagePercentPerDepth = 15
    MonsterCountPercentPerDepth = 20

    ; The boss climbs faster than its underlings — it is meant to be the wall.
    BossHealthPercentPerDepth = 40
    BossDamagePercentPerDepth = 25

    ; Experience is worth more the deeper it was earned, so levelling roughly keeps
    ; pace with what it is levelling against.
    ExperiencePercentPerDepth = 30
  End
End
